 you know  logically um  you have to be thinking as as if you  were  giving this sort of command to a  computer  as for example when you're writing a  program you have to make sure that your  logic is absolutely impeccable  and imagine that you're asking computers  to return a value for you you just  you just  wouldn't want to  make anything fuzzy and  and doubtful there and also make sure  that you use very clear feedback  feedback whenever possible  um okay um  furthermore um  we really like to control our  surroundings to maybe cope with anxiety  or  apply some  strict wacky habits of our own or our  personal roles to the world  so  i i know this is fairly rare  because i don't see that very often but  when you give a control  over presentation such as choosing a  color theme or adjusting the saturation  that really makes things easier  oh and also  try and not impose your own font size  from the user just  um allow them to resize it in the  browser that's that's always  uh  that always wins our hearts too  um  having said that  you really might want to  think of users with asperger's  as your ultimate ideal perfect testers  for your apps and for your products in  terms of  usability and accessibility  first of all  we're brutally honest especially when  asked a very direct question where less  prone to lying  we loved correct  we're really obsessed about  detail and controlling everything  and  we're also like super sensitive and  we exaggerate and overreact over partic  over everything really so what bothers  a normal  uh person really bothers a person with  asperger's like a hundred times more  so  you've got a very very sensitive  and very fine-tuned instrument of a  tester there possibly  the takeaway from all of this is that  accessibility for users with cognitive  impairments  translates itself directly to  just general usability for everyone  and  much of what i've said might appear  obvious but  once you really grasp  these really basic guidelines you really  might arrive at a very usable and very  accessible product for absolutely  everyone  so you really want to think about  accessibility  for cognitive impairments at every  single stage  during development during design  so  like i said whether you're a developer  or a designer  this is what you should be thinking  about and  that is what i wanted to say and let me  just add one more thing um looking back  at the history of  web design and development and app app  development  i  do think that we're on the right path  because years ago everything was very  much inaccessible  and  now the web is just beautiful and apps  are beautiful and it fills me with like  with childlike wonder  because there are very clear visual cues  everything is  minimalistic everything is simple and  while these things might appear quite  natural  and obvious you guys  as designers and developers are really  life savers and  uh  you really do make the web a friendly  place and it all proves that the  very existence of the ui and ux movement  um  is really doing a great job  so yes thank you so much you can follow  me on twitter at battalion thank you for  having me  you
 

